<h2>FAQ</h2>

<p style="text-align: center;">
	Answers to some of the questions I hear most often. If you don't see yours here, feel free to reach out.
</p>

<div class="faq-list">
	<details class="faq-item reveal">
		<summary>Are you willing to wear a mask in session?</summary>
		<div class="faq-answer">
			<p>
				I am always happy to wear a mask in session. At the start of services, I will ask your preference about
				whether you’d like me to wear a mask or not. For some people, they prefer to have me wear a mask to
				mitigate the risk of getting ill (this is especially important for people who are, or live with someone
				who is, immunocompromised). Others prefer I do not have a mask so they can better see my face during
				therapy. I am happy to do whatever you are most comfortable with.
			</p>
			<p>
				If I am ill on a day that we have a session, I will be sure to inform you so you can determine whether
				you’d like to move to a virtual appointment, reschedule, or meet with masks.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you are ill on a day that we have a session, I ask that you inform me as soon as possible so we can
				determine what option makes the most sense to keep us both safe.
			</p>
		</div>
	</details>

	<details class="faq-item reveal">
		<summary>Can I cuss in session?</summary>
		<div class="faq-answer">
			<p>
				Absolutely.
			</p>
		</div>
	</details>

	<details class="faq-item reveal">
		<summary>What is your cancellation policy?</summary>
		<div class="faq-answer">
			<p>
				We will discuss my cancellation policy during our initial session in detail - I never want people to
				feel surprised about any of my policies or procedures. In short, here is my general policy:
			</p>
			<p>
				If you cancel your appointment within 24 hours of the session, there is a $100 cancellation charge.
			</p>
		</div>
	</details>

	<details class="faq-item reveal">
		<summary>Who do you speak to about our sessions?</summary>
		<div class="faq-answer">
			<p>
				I never speak to anyone about you unless necessary. Here are some times in which I may need to speak to
				someone about you:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Insurance:</strong> If I am billing insurance, I may need to provide enough detail to the
					insurance company to justify their ongoing authorization of services. I only share what I determine
					to be necessary for your insurance company to have an understanding of why services may be
					clinically necessary.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Your emergency contact:</strong> If I believe you are a danger to yourself or others, I may
					need to contact your emergency contact. Whenever possible, I will let you know if I determine this
					to be necessary, and we can contact them together if you’d prefer. During your first appointment,
					you will let me know who your emergency contact is.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Duty to warn:</strong> If I have reasonable suspicion that you are a danger to another
					person, I may have an ethical and legal “duty to warn” that individual.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Trusted colleagues:</strong> As a clinician, I value ongoing consultation with colleagues
					that abide by the same (or similar) code of ethics that I do. I have strong, relevant experience and
					training, however I am not an expert in every situation. I regularly consult with colleagues about
					cases to ensure I am providing holistic, thoughtful support to you and/or your family. I never share
					protected health information (your name, school, date of birth, etc.), and only share pertinent
					information relevant to the consultation/question at hand.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</details>
</div>
